    { id: 'satfat', short: 'Butter & sat. fat', name: 'Butter & saturated fat', sub: 'Butter, ghee, coconut oil, tallow, fatty meat', verdict: 'limit', certainty: 'moderate',
      one: `"Butter is back" made headlines in 2014. It never really came back.`,
      told: { pos: 1, who: 'TIME cover 2014; coconut-oil marketing', text: `"Eat butter. Coconut oil is a superfood."` },
      now: { pos: -1, text: `Raises LDL cholesterol; swapping it for unsaturated oils lowers heart risk.` },
      science: [
        [`The American Heart Association found that replacing saturated with polyunsaturated fat cut heart disease by about 30% in trials.`, SRC.ahaSFA],
        [`Coconut oil raised LDL cholesterol compared with plant oils in trials.`, SRC.coconut],
        [`More butter was linked to 15% higher death rates over 33 years.`, SRC.zhangButter],
        [`The food matters: saturated fat in cheese and yoghurt seems less harmful than in butter or fatty meat.`, SRC.dehghan]
      ],
      oz: `Coconut oil was a 2010s supermarket craze in Australia too. Australia never banned trans fats outright; levels here are low by voluntary reformulation.`,
      tip: `Use olive or canola oil for most cooking. Keep butter and coconut oil for taste, not health.` },
    { id: 'salt', name: 'Salt', sub: 'Sodium, mostly hidden in bread, processed meat and takeaway', verdict: 'limit', certainty: 'strong',
      one: `A huge trial led from Sydney went a long way to settling the "salt wars": less sodium means fewer strokes.`,
      told: { pos: 0, who: 'Salt and processed-food industry', text: `"Salt is harmless, and low-salt diets are dangerous."` },
      now: { pos: -1, text: `Too much raises blood pressure; cutting it, or swapping to potassium salt, prevents strokes.` },
      science: [
        [`In a five-year trial of 20,995 people, swapping salt for a potassium salt substitute cut strokes by 14% and deaths by 12%.`, SRC.ssass],
        [`The WHO recommends under 5 g of salt a day. The world average is about 10.8 g.`, SRC.whoSalt]
      ],
      oz: `The salt substitute trial (SSaSS) was led by Sydney's George Institute and funded by the NHMRC. Most salt Australians eat comes from bread, processed meat and takeaway, not the shaker.`,
      tip: `If you have high blood pressure, ask your GP about potassium salt substitutes. Most people with kidney disease shouldn't use them.` },
    { id: 'formula', name: 'Baby formula', sub: 'Infant formula and toddler milks', verdict: 'depends', certainty: 'strong',
      one: `Formula is safe and vital for many babies. The marketing, and some factories, are the problem.`,
      told: { pos: 1.5, who: 'Formula marketing', text: `"Closer than ever to breast milk. Boosts brain development."` },
      now: { pos: 0.5, text: `A safe substitute when needed. Most "brain" and "immunity" claims lack good evidence; toddler milks are unnecessary.` },
      science: [
        [`A 2023 Lancet series found that the US$55 billion formula industry uses marketing that exploits parents' worries, with health claims that lack good evidence.`, SRC.lancetFormula],
        [`The 1981 WHO marketing code bans promoting formula to parents. Most countries only partly enforce it.`, SRC.lancetFormula]
      ],
      oz: `Australia's formula marketing agreement (MAIF) is voluntary, and it doesn't cover toddler milks, which are heavily advertised.`,
      tip: `Fed is best. If you use formula, a basic standard formula is as good as a premium one. Toddlers over 12 months don't need toddler milk.` },
    { id: 'cereal', short: 'Cereal & snacks', name: 'Cereal & "healthy" snacks', sub: 'Breakfast cereals, kids’ snacks, drink powders', verdict: 'depends', certainty: 'moderate',
      one: `The home of the health halo: stars, ticks and claims printed on boxes of sugar.`,
      told: { pos: 1.5, who: 'Cereal marketing', text: `"Part of a healthy breakfast. Improves attentiveness. Supports immunity."` },
      now: { pos: -0.5, text: `High-fibre wholegrain cereals are good; many kids' cereals and snacks are a quarter to two-thirds sugar.` },
      science: [
        [`Higher fibre and wholegrain intakes are linked to 15–30% less heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and bowel cancer.`, SRC.reynolds],
        [`The FTC found Kellogg's "improves attentiveness by nearly 20%" claim was false. The company's own study didn't show it.`, ['FTC v Kellogg, 2009', 'https://www.ftc.gov/node/47083']]
      ],
      oz: `Milo carried 4.5 Health Stars because it was rated as if made with skim milk; on its own the powder scored 1.5. Only 13% of people drank it that way.`,
      tip: `Check the fibre and the sugar, not the stars. Plain oats, muesli without added sugar, or wholegrain biscuits.` },
    { id: 'coffee', name: 'Coffee & tea', sub: 'The good news', verdict: 'good', certainty: 'moderate',
      one: `Once suspected of causing heart disease and cancer, now one of the most consistently "protective" drinks.`,
      told: { pos: -1, who: 'Health advice, 1980s–1990s', text: `"Bad for your heart, and possibly carcinogenic."` },
      now: { pos: 1, text: `3–4 cups a day is linked to lower death rates, less liver disease and less type 2 diabetes.` },
      science: [
        [`A review of 200+ meta-analyses found 3–4 cups a day linked to the lowest risk of death, heart disease and liver cancer. Caution in pregnancy.`, SRC.poole],
        [`IARC removed coffee from its "possibly carcinogenic" list in 2016. Only very hot drinks (over about 65 °C) are flagged.`, SRC.iarcCoffee]
      ],
      oz: `Australia's café culture runs on milk-based coffee. The milk doesn't cancel the benefits; the syrups and sugar can.`,
      tip: `Enjoy it. Avoid too much late in the day, and go easy if you're pregnant.` }
